# Issue-first workflow

Every change must be tracked by a GitHub issue before the first commit. Close the issue only after the PR is merged and post-merge checks succeed — never on commit alone.

## 1. Open issue before committing

Create a GitHub issue describing the problem, goal, and acceptance criteria **before** creating a branch or making commits.

## 2. Reference the issue in branch and commits

- Branch name: include the issue number when practical (e.g. `fix/42-pin-signature`).
- Commit messages: link the issue with `Refs #N` while work is in progress.
- Use `Fixes #N` or `Closes #N` only when the commit/PR should auto-close the issue on merge.

Example:

```
fix: pin go-api-signature to v0.3.12

Refs #42
```

## 3. Open PR linking to the issue

Reference the issue in the PR title or description (e.g. `Refs #42` or `Fixes #42`). Keep the issue updated with scope changes, blockers, review feedback, and CI failures — do not rely on the PR description alone.

## 4. Close issue only after merge and green pipelines

1. Merge the PR.
2. Confirm GitHub Actions (and any release or deploy workflows) completed successfully on `main`.
3. If pipelines fail on `main`, keep or reopen the issue, fix forward, and re-verify.
4. Close the issue only after post-merge pipelines are green.

**Do not close the issue when committing or opening the PR.**

## Agent checklist

- [ ] Issue exists before first commit
- [ ] Branch and commits reference the issue (`Refs #N` / `Fixes #N`)
- [ ] PR links to the issue
- [ ] Issue updated during review and CI
- [ ] Post-merge pipeline status verified on `main`
- [ ] Issue closed only after clean post-merge runs
